{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: make an MCP session able to address an actor added after it started. Third filing, per your intake answer on #10937: this one rests solely on the fresh cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact at main 1ddf4ffb and restates the finding in full here rather than leaning on the stale assert, so nothing under it is flared. THE FINDING, self-contained: ResolveActorAddress at internal/app/app.go resolves a request's body.to against w.Config.Actors, the local config file; the MCP adapter's attach caches the opened room per path for the life of the process, so that config is read at process start and never again, and a long-lived adapter addresses a start-time roster. Observed directly: a planner adapter up for two days refused @ox-checker and its full fingerprint with \"unknown actor address\" while gs state --server against the same resident accepted the identical fingerprint seconds later; config.json on disk listed the actor. The trust choice is right and stays — resolve against the local config custody record, not the durable projection; the defect is the cached lifetime. The error message matters as much as the fix: a failure to resolve must be distinguishable from a roster that has not been re-read, so a caller can never report a live actor as unknown. You confirmed the conditions read as current and that you will schedule and claim this filing.","body":{"area":"cmd/gitseq-mcp","conditions":"An MCP adapter session must be able to address an actor added to the workroom after that session started, without restarting the adapter. Keep address resolution against the local config custody record rather than the durable projection; the defect is the cached lifetime, not the source. Decide and state whether the fix is to re-read config on resolution failure, to detect config change, or to stop caching the workspace, and say why the chosen shape does not reintroduce a per-call filesystem cost on the hot path. Distinguish in the returned error between an address that resolves against no known actor and one that failed after a re-read, so a caller can never report a live actor as unknown. Add a mutation-sensitive test that an actor added after the workspace is opened resolves without reopening, and one pinning the two error cases apart; prove each fails when its guard alone is removed. Run gofmt, go vet ./..., go test ./... -count=1 and a race run over the affected packages at the exact head. Publish exact-head artifacts for every changed stable path and obtain an independent review with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions before merge.","severity":"medium","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}